      message":"{\"summary\":\"Architecture memo — recommendation: build a macOS 13+ LSUIElement
      utility using an AppKit-owned NSStatusItem and NSMenu, with SwiftUI limited to Settings/About.
      NSStatusItem is the boring, robust choice: it gives explicit lifetime, menu rebuilding,
      target/action handling, accessibility, and status-button control. MenuBarExtra is simpler, but
      Apple documents that a menu-only app may terminate if its extra is removed. Sources:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsstatusitem and
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/menubarextra\\n\\nArchitecture: keep
      WorkspaceID, WorkspaceSnapshot, lifecycle state, command results, and transition rules in a
      Foundation-only domain module. Put process execution and status collection behind an MSWClient
      actor; expose state through an @MainActor AppModel; let StatusMenuController only render
      snapshots and dispatch typed intents. Inject CommandRunning, WorkspaceOpening,
      NotificationSending, LoginItemManaging, and Clock protocols.\\n\\nCLI boundary: use `msw
      start|stop|restart WORKSPACE` for every mutation so the app preserves the repository’s
      Keychain, quarantine, readiness, validation, and timeout behavior. The current `msw status` is
      human-formatted, so never scrape it. The verified underlying query `msb status -a --label
      msw.managed=true --format json` returns a JSON array containing name, status, CPU, memory,
      image, and ports. The preferred integration contract is a future minimal `msw status --format
      json` pass-through; until then, direct `msb` JSON access is acceptable only for read-only
      monitoring. Decode with Codable, tolerate additional fields, map unknown statuses explicitly,
      and treat missing expected workspaces as unavailable rather than stopped.\\n\\nExecution and
      environment: resolve the executable in this order: saved explicit path, `~/.local/bin/msw`
      installed by setup.sh, then known PATH directories. Resolve `~` with FileManager, verify
      regular executable files, and run `msw version` as a health check. Build a deterministic PATH
      including the executable directory, `~/.local/bin`, `/opt/homebrew/bin`, `/usr/local/bin`, and
      system directories. Never use `sh -c`, `zsh -lc`, source shell profiles, parse config.sh, or
      concatenate user input into shell text. Pass every workspace, path, port, and scheme as a
      separate Process argument. Capture stdout and stderr concurrently with bounded buffers,
      preserve exit code and termination reason, redact logs, and never log the environment.
      Foundation Process inherits its launcher’s environment:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/process\\n\\nPolling and concurrency:
      lifecycle status has no suitable stable event stream; `metrics --watch` is unrelated and
      should not drive VM state. Use a single-flight adaptive poll: approximately 2 seconds while
      the menu is open or a transition is pending, 10 seconds while idle, plus immediate refresh at
      launch, menu opening, wake, and command completion. Observe wake through NSWorkspace’s
      notification center. Preserve the last good snapshot and mark it stale after failures.
      Serialize mutations per workspace in an actor, disable conflicting actions, and represent
      starting/stopping optimistically without claiming success before a confirming poll. Tasks
      belong to the application model, not menu views, so closing the menu does not cancel a VM
      operation. Cancel polling promptly; normally let lifecycle mutations finish. If enforcing hard
      timeouts, terminate and reap the Process, then kill after a grace period; use a process-group
      runner if descendant termination must be guaranteed. Source:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsworkspace/didwakenotification\\n\\nLaunch
      at login: use SMAppService.mainApp.register/unregister, surface enabled, notRegistered,
      requiresApproval, and notFound states, and link to Login Items settings when approval is
      required. Do not add a helper target or custom LaunchAgent. Register only after the app is
      installed at a stable path. Sources:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/servicemanagement/smappservice/mainapp and
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/servicemanagement/smappservice/register()\\n\\nSandb
      ox and signing: disable App Sandbox for this personal utility. A sandboxed child inherits
      restrictions, cannot freely traverse the user’s CLI/config/SSH locations, and NSWorkspace
      ignores launch arguments from sandboxed callers. A Mac App Store build is therefore a poor fit
      unless the architecture is replaced by a bundled helper and explicit user-selected resources.
      Keep Hardened Runtime enabled, sign with Developer ID, notarize and staple direct
      distributions, and avoid unnecessary exception entitlements. The app itself must never read
      msw’s GitHub tokens; the existing CLI remains their owner. Sources:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/protecting-user-data-with-app-sandbox,
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsworkspace/openconfiguration/arguments, and
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/notarizing-macos-software-before-distributi
      on\\n\\nNotifications: request UNUserNotificationCenter authorization only from an explicit
      Settings toggle and install its delegate during launch. Notify only after an initial baseline,
      for deduplicated meaningful transitions such as an unexpected running-to-stopped change or a
      user-requested operation failure. Suppress notifications caused by the app’s own stop command.
      Notification actions should carry validated workspace identifiers and dispatch typed intents;
      never embed arbitrary commands. Source:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/unusernotificationcenter\\n\\nExte
      rnal opening: centralize app discovery with NSWorkspace and bundle identifiers. For Terminal,
      open an `ssh://root@WORKSPACE.msb` URL specifically with Terminal; the repository’s SSH
      ProxyCommand starts a stopped VM, though this path opens the guest home rather than a
      requested `/workspace` subdirectory. Avoid AppleScript and its Automation permission. For
      Ghostty, locate `com.mitchellh.ghostty` and launch a new application instance with
      argument-array semantics equivalent to `-e <resolved-msw> WORKSPACE [PATH]`; local Ghostty
      help confirms macOS CLI launch should go through app launching. For Zed, execute `msw zed
      WORKSPACE [PATH]`, preserving its readiness and SSH URI behavior. For browsers, run `msw start
      WORKSPACE`, obtain the validated URL from `msw url`, validate its scheme/host/port, then call
      NSWorkspace.open. If an app-specific URL scheme is later added, allow-list actions and
      workspaces and never accept a raw command. Source:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/nsworkspace\\n\\nTestability: unit-test JSON
      fixtures including unknown fields/statuses, missing workspaces, malformed output, stale-state
      behavior, transition deduplication, polling coalescence, cancellation, timeouts, and
      per-workspace serialization. Test environment discovery against fake homes and executable
      paths. Use fake command runners to assert exact executable/argument arrays, including
      hostile-looking paths that must remain single arguments. Add integration tests with temporary
      fake `msw` and `msb` executables, plus focused menu-controller tests using injected snapshots.
      Keep real VM, login-item, notification, browser, and application launching behind opt-in smoke
      tests.\\n\\nVerification: inspected README.md, config.sh, bin/msw, setup.sh, command
      documentation, existing fake-backed tests, installed command help, and read-only JSON status
      output. Confirmed there is currently no Swift/Xcode app. No files were edited and no
      project-wide tests, formatters, or linters were run. The working tree’s existing unrelated
      changes were preserved. Posted the completed design milestone to Gazette:
